    "summary": "Solid love‑themed set with strong Sugaree and Ramble On Rose",
    "blurb": "The first set shines on Sugaree, Ramble On Rose and The Promised Land, while the second set features a heartfelt Foolish Heart jam. Jerry is mostly on fire, backed by tight drumming from Mickey and Billy. An audience recording captures the energy despite some mixed source quality.",
    "review": "On September 17, 1993 at Madison Square Garden the Dead delivered a love‑themed marathon that still feels fresh for fans hunting late‑’90s gems. The show opens with a crisp Jack Straw segueing into a soaring Sugaree, where Jerry Garcia is described as “on” all night, his lead lines cutting through the mix. Ramble On Rose follows with Bob Weir adding tasteful background soloing and the dual drums of Mickey Hart and Bill Kreutzmann locking in tight, driving grooves that lift the entire set. The Promised Land rounds out a first‑set that many listeners recall as solid and uplifting.\nThe second set starts strong with Foolish Heart, the only jam that survived the mixed reviews, and then drifts through a series of standards—Man Smart, Woman Smarter, Ship Of Fools (noted as a weak spot), and a Saint Of Circumstance‑Jam‑Drums‑Space sequence that builds into an uplifting I Need A Miracle. The band’s chemistry stays intact through the extended Space, leading into a soulful Turn On Your Lovelight for the encore. While one soundboard source felt “half‑in, half‑out” for Jerry, the audience tape praised the overall performance and captures the night’s vibe without major dropouts, making it the recommended listening choice.",
